WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.999
Film Courage: This question is from the Youtube
channel Tall Guy NH “What are some top things

00:00:03.999 --> 00:00:06.290
director do or don’t do that drive you crazy?”

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.792
Lawrence Jordan, ACE and Owner of Master The
Workflow: One of the big things is that a

00:00:08.792 --> 00:00:14.010
director talks over the performance and will
be coaching the actor to do certain things

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:20.240
during a performance and they will be stepping
on each other’s lines.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:27.409
An extension of that is when actors are stepping
over each other, stepping on each other’s

00:00:27.409 --> 00:00:38.360
lines, the director won’t sort of gently
try to be aware of your overlaps, overlaps

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.100
are a pain in the butt.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.880
They can be dealt with but quite often they
just can’t and they will just have to be

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.680
looped so that is a couple.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:55.410
Film Courage: What about if it’s intentional
where they are having an argument and interrupting

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.830
each other, they are both angry.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:07.509
Lawrence: Yeah I mean that just sort of comes
down to sort of finely finessing your dialogue

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:13.240
edits and attaching they may be part of the
first part of the word that is overlapped

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.310
to the second part of the word that is clean
on the other side.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:25.850
There are all kinds of tricks you can work
around it with but some of the time you are

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:27.539
just going to have to loop it anyway.

00:01:27.539 --> 00:01:36.840
That’s a drag because the performances are
because unless you’ve got a great looper

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.270
they are usually never as good.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.520
Film Courage: This question is from the Youtube
channel Robert Knees “Differences in editing

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.110
for single-camera or multi-camera production?”

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:49.759
Lawrence: Well I’ve worked on a lot of films
with multiple cameras which I’ve never worked

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:58.340
on like a three-camera comedy kind of thing
so I can only address working with multiple

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.340
cameras.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.530
I love working with multiple cameras.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:16.900
It just gives you more choices, it makes matching
easier a lot of the times.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:25.549
When stuff is shot with a single camera a
lot of times frankly you are going to have

00:02:25.549 --> 00:02:28.340
to work harder to make it work sometimes.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:34.069
Again, this depends on the actor, it depends
on the director, it depends on the day.

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:40.319
There are so many variables in making a film.

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:44.239
I love multi-camera.

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:49.730
Sometimes when it gets to be five or six or
seven cameras it gets to be a bit overwhelming

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:55.730
but they keep making those cameras smaller
and smaller and they keep putting them in

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.739
the strangest of places so it’s just what
us editors have to deal with.

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:01.150
Question For The Viewers: What are some other
mistakes a director makes that upset the editor?

